diagnosis_text:
The fabric is healthy end-to-end. The BGP control plane on both spines and leaves is up, and data-plane forwarding between hosts works. From the endpoints, reachability tests show pc_0_0 → pc_0_1 and pc_0_1 → pc_0_0 both succeed with 0% loss and ~1.2–1.3 ms RTT. On the spines, BGP sessions are established: spine_router_0_0 (AS65100) has three neighbors up (172.16.0.0 AS65000, 172.16.0.5 AS65200, 172.16.0.7 AS65201) and is receiving prefixes from both leaves; spine_router_0_1 (AS65101) likewise has all three neighbors up and is receiving two prefixes from each. On the leaves, routing tables show the expected remote LANs learned via BGP: leaf_router_0_0 installs 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.4 on eth0, and leaf_router_0_1 installs 10.0.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.6 on eth0. That means traffic from each host is properly forwarded toward its directly connected leaf, up to the spines, and down to the opposite leaf.

Mechanism and localization therefore contradict the reported symptom: both spines are operational, BGP adjacencies are stable, and both leaves have correct next-hops via their spine-facing interfaces. Root cause: no current fault found; the report was likely transient or a misdiagnosis.

Corrective fix: no device restart is required. Do not reboot spine_router_0_0. If there was a time-bound incident overnight, review FRR and system logs for flaps; otherwise, close the ticket after confirming with the user that service is restored. 
